+# Implementation Plan: Restore Displaced Rustdoc Blocks
+
+**Branch**: `main` | **Date**: 2026-10-26 | **Spec**: [spec.md](spec.md)
+
+**Input**: Feature specification from `/specs/001-bench/spec.md`
+
+**Note**: This plan was filled in by the `speckit.plan` command; its definition describes the execution workflow.
+
+## Summary
+
+A recent merge inserted two new functions into the middle of pre-existing rustdoc blocks, so three
+functions now present another function's prose and one of them presents an error contract its
+signature cannot produce. The feature relocates three documentation blocks back directly above the
+items they describe:
+
+1. `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs` — move the `addressable_scope` block (opening
+   "The ONE scope the `execution` verbs can address for a given execution id.", including the
+   `# Errors` paragraph) from above `fn repository_scope` to directly above `fn addressable_scope`;
+   leave `repository_scope`'s own block ("The same addressing rule as [`addressable_scope`] …")
+   above `fn repository_scope`.
+2. `apps/cli/src/commands/mcp/tools.rs` — move the "Closed like every other schema here…" block
+   from above `fn assign_schema` to directly above `fn synthesize_schema`; leave `assign_schema`
+   undocumented.
+
+The technical approach is comment-line relocation only: every documentation line moves verbatim, no
+code token changes, no code reordering, no reformatting, no other file touched. The smallest
+adequate observers are a per-site rustdoc-adjacency inspection plus a scoped `rustfmt` formatting
+check on the two files (not a workspace-wide build or full test suite). See [research.md](research.md).
+
