+# Research: Restore Displaced Rustdoc Blocks
+
+**Feature**: 001-bench | **Phase**: 0 | **Branch**: `main`
+
+The Technical Context in [plan.md](plan.md) carried **no `NEEDS CLARIFICATION`** — every language,
+dependency, platform, and scope value is fixed by the spec and the repository's pinned toolchain.
+This phase therefore has no open unknowns to resolve; it records the two judgments the plan depends
+on: how to prove the fix (smallest adequate observer) and how to determine which block owns which
+item when two blocks sit stacked above one function. Each is written as a decision with rationale
+and the alternatives considered, per the consolidation format.
+
+## R-1: Smallest adequate observer for a comment-only relocation
+
+**Context**: Journey-Proven Development requires proof proportional to the change and forbids one
+universal testing ritual. Keel classifies docs/comment edits as the direct route that needs "nothing
+beyond the task record when the change has a known observer." The user brief explicitly caps the work:
+verify with the smallest check that observes the change; do **not** run a workspace-wide build or the
+full test suite.
+
+**Decision**: Prove the feature with three local observers, none of which compiles the whole
+workspace or runs the test suite:
+
+1. **Per-site rustdoc-adjacency inspection.** For each of the four affected items, confirm the exact
+   set of doc lines immediately preceding its `fn` signature matches the item it was written for.
+   This directly observes FR-001…FR-004 and SC-001 (correct ownership), which is the entire value of
+   the change. Concretely: `addressable_scope` is preceded only by its "The ONE scope …" block
+   including `# Errors`; `repository_scope` only by "The same addressing rule as [`addressable_scope`]
+   …"; `synthesize_schema` only by "Closed like every other schema here …"; `assign_schema` by no doc.
+2. **Scoped formatting check.** Run `cargo +1.97.1 fmt --check` on the two touched files only and
+   confirm it reports no required change (SC-003), i.e. the move introduced no doubled blank lines
+   and dropped no separating blank line.
+3. **Byte-scoped diff against the pre-fix state.** A `git diff --stat`/`git diff` scoped to the two
+   files shows the only differences are the relocated comment lines (FR-005, FR-006, SC-002): no other
+   file differs and no code line changed.
+
+Optionally, to observe FR-007 ("generated documentation is factually correct"), `cargo +1.97.1 doc -p
+graphhelm-cli --no-deps` may be used as an extra cross-check, but it is not required: adjacency
+inspection already proves each block sits on the fallible/infallible item that fits it.
+
+**Rationale**: rustdoc attaches a doc comment to the single item immediately below it. Relocating a
+block so it is the contiguous run of `///` lines directly above the correct `fn` is, by construction,
+both necessary and sufficient for correct attachment; no compiled behavior is involved, so a build or
+test run adds no evidence about what the feature actually promises (correct documentation ownership).
+This keeps the proof exactly as large as the defect, satisfying both SC-002/SC-003 and the no-universal-ritual
+rule.
+
+**Alternatives considered**:
+- *Workspace-wide `cargo test` + full gate (`ci/gate.ps1`).* Rejected: it observes behavior this
+  change cannot affect, is far heavier than warranted, and the brief forbids it. It still holds as a
+  backstop if a wider delivery gate is run later (SC-004: previously-passing tests keep passing).
+- *Relying on rustdoc generation alone.* Rejected as the sole observer: it confirms the text rendered
+  but requires compiling the crate and does not, by itself, assert the byte-scoped "nothing else
+  changed" property (SC-002) that a scoped diff gives directly.
+
+## R-2: Deciding ownership of the two stacked blocks above `repository_scope`
+
+**Context**: In `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs` the post-merge state stacks two contiguous
+blocks directly above `fn repository_scope`: first the "The ONE scope …" block ending in an
+`# Errors` paragraph (lines 208–218), then "The same addressing rule as [`addressable_scope`] …"
+(lines 219–221). Rustdoc treats all of it as `repository_scope`'s doc, and `addressable_scope`
+(line 230) currently carries none. Ownership must be assigned so each block returns to its own item.
+
+**Decision**: Assign blocks by content fit to signature/body:
+- The "The ONE scope …" block belongs to **`addressable_scope`**. It ends in `# Errors: [`Failure`]
+  when `execution` is not a valid identifier.` That error contract is producible only by
+  `addressable_scope(execution: &str) -> Result<RepositoryScope, Failure>`, whose body actually calls
+  `ExecutionId::parse(execution).map_err(...)?`. It references a `&str execution` argument that
+  `repository_scope()` (no parameters, infallible) has. So the `# Errors` paragraph cannot describe
+  the inserted function.
+- The "The same addressing rule as [`addressable_scope`] …" block belongs to **`repository_scope`**.
+  Its first line names `addressable_scope` as the thing being compared against — a reference that only
+  makes sense from a sibling, not from `addressable_scope` itself — and it describes a repository
+  rather than an execution stream, matching `repository_scope`'s `None` execution slot. The intra-module
+  `[`addressable_scope`]` link stays valid because both items remain in the same module (spec edge case).
+
+For `apps/cli/src/commands/mcp/tools.rs`: the "Closed like every other schema here. `fixture` and
+`route` are both optional…" block explains the `fixture` and `route` options. Those keys exist only in
+`synthesize_schema`'s body (lines 772–781); the inserted `assign_schema`'s body builds a schema over
+`executionId`/`node`/`actorId` and contains neither. So the block belongs to `synthesize_schema`, and
+`assign_schema` is left undocumented (the pre-merge state) per the spec's out-of-scope note.
+
+**Rationale**: Content fit is dispositive and unambiguous here — each disputed block names an option,
+an argument, or an error that only one of the adjacent functions has. This restores the pre-merge
+state the spec defines as the target, and it keeps the move to pure relocation (FR-005) with no
+rewording needed to make a block "fit."
+
+**Alternatives considered**:
+- *Reassigning `assign_schema`/`repository_scope` to carry the larger block.* Rejected: it would attach
+  a doc describing options/errors a function does not produce (violating FR-007) or would require
+  rewording to force a fit (violating FR-005).
+- *Authoring fresh docs for the two inserted functions.* Rejected: explicitly out of scope — the fix
+  restores the pre-merge state, in which they were undocumented / minimally documented as-is.
+
+## Open questions
+
+None. All `NEEDS CLARIFICATION` items were absent at intake; both plan-load-bearing judgments are
+resolved above and are verifiable from the two source files without any external dependency.
