+# Feature Specification: Restore Displaced Rustdoc Blocks
+
+**Feature Branch**: `001-bench`
+
+**Created**: 2026-10-26
+
+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "A merge inserted `repository_scope` between `addressable_scope`'s doc block and `addressable_scope` in `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs`, and `assign_schema` between `synthesize_schema`'s doc block and `synthesize_schema` in `apps/cli/src/commands/mcp/tools.rs`. rustdoc now attaches those docs to the wrong items. Move each displaced doc block back directly above its own function. Change comments only: no code, no reformatting, no other files."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Every function reads its own documentation (Priority: P1)
+
+A maintainer or contributor opens the CLI source, or the documentation generated from it, for the execution-command helper module or the MCP tool-schema module. Because a recent merge inserted new functions in the middle of existing documentation blocks, some functions currently present another function's prose — and one of them presents an error contract that its signature cannot produce. After this change, every affected function is preceded by exactly its own documentation, or by none.
+
+**Why this priority**: Documentation attached to the wrong item is a correctness defect: a reader may trust an error contract a function cannot produce, or a schema builder presents the rationale of its neighbor. Restoring the blocks to their owning functions is the entire value of this feature.
+
+**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by inspecting — or generating documentation from — the two named source files: the block describing `addressable_scope` appears only directly above `addressable_scope`, the block describing `repository_scope` only directly above `repository_scope`, and the block describing `synthesize_schema` only directly above `synthesize_schema`.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the post-merge state where `repository_scope` sits between `addressable_scope`'s documentation block and `addressable_scope` itself, **When** the fix is applied, **Then** `addressable_scope`'s whole block — including its `# Errors` paragraph — sits directly above `addressable_scope`, uninterrupted by any other item, and no other item in the file carries any line of that block.
+2. **Given** the same state, **When** the fix is applied, **Then** `repository_scope`'s own block ("The same addressing rule as …") sits directly above `repository_scope`.
+3. **Given** the state where `assign_schema` sits between `synthesize_schema`'s documentation block and `synthesize_schema`, **When** the fix is applied, **Then** the block beginning "Closed like every other schema here…" sits directly above `synthesize_schema`, and `assign_schema` carries no borrowed documentation.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - The change stays as small as the defect (Priority: P2)
+
+A reviewer examines the change and needs assurance that the fix carries no collateral: no rewording, no code-token changes, no code reordering, no reformatting, and no other files touched.
+
+**Why this priority**: This repository's delivery rules require proof proportional to the change; a comment-only fix is credible only if it can be shown to touch nothing but comment lines.
+
+**Independent Test**: Can be tested by comparing each of the two modified files against its pre-fix state: the only difference is the relocated documentation lines, byte for byte.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the pre-fix and post-fix states of either modified file, **When** they are compared, **Then** every byte except the relocated documentation lines is identical, and no file anywhere else in the repository differs.
+
+---
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- **What happens when a moved block references the item it moves past?** `repository_scope`'s block names `addressable_scope`. The reference must remain valid after the move (both items stay in the same module); the move adds, removes, and rewrites no references.
+- **How is ownership of a disputed block decided?** By content fit: the `# Errors` paragraph fits the one fallible function (`addressable_scope` returns a failure), not the infallible `repository_scope`; the schema block explains `fixture`/`route` options that exist only in `synthesize_schema`'s body, while the inserted `assign_schema`'s body contains neither.
+- **What happens to surrounding whitespace?** Relocating a block must not leave doubled blank lines or drop the single blank line that separates adjacent items, so a formatting check over the file reports no required change.
+- **Does `assign_schema` need its own new documentation?** Out of scope: the fix restores the pre-merge state, in which `assign_schema` was undocumented. Authoring new documentation is a separate task.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: In `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs`, the documentation block describing `addressable_scope` (opening "The ONE scope the `execution` verbs can address for a given execution id." and containing the `# Errors` section) MUST sit directly above `fn addressable_scope`, with no intervening item.
+- **FR-002**: In `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs`, the documentation block describing `repository_scope` ("The same addressing rule as [`addressable_scope`] …") MUST sit directly above `fn repository_scope`.
+- **FR-003**: In `apps/cli/src/commands/mcp/tools.rs`, the documentation block opening "Closed like every other schema here." MUST sit directly above `fn synthesize_schema`.
+- **FR-004**: After the change, NO item in either file MUST carry a documentation line written for another item; specifically, `assign_schema` MUST carry no doc comment, and `repository_scope` MUST NOT carry the `# Errors` paragraph.
+- **FR-005**: The fix MUST relocate existing documentation lines only: every documentation line's text is preserved verbatim — no word added, removed, or reworded.
+- **FR-006**: The fix MUST NOT change any non-comment token, MUST NOT reorder any non-comment line, MUST NOT reformat any line, and MUST NOT touch any file other than the two named above.
+- **FR-007**: After the fix, each item's attached documentation MUST be consistent with the item's actual signature and body (for example, an `# Errors` section appears only above an item that can fail), so generated documentation is factually correct for all four affected items.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: 4 of 4 affected items (`addressable_scope`, `repository_scope`, `assign_schema`, `synthesize_schema`) carry exactly the right documentation: zero documentation lines attached to the wrong item, verified by generated documentation or direct adjacency inspection.
+- **SC-002**: A byte-level comparison of each modified file against its pre-fix state shows only the relocated documentation lines as differences — zero other differences in those files and zero differences elsewhere in the repository.
+- **SC-003**: A formatting check over the modified files reports no required change (the files are otherwise byte-identical).
+- **SC-004**: Every test that passed before the change passes after it with identical results — the change is behavior-neutral.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- The two inserted functions arrived through the merge without their own documentation blocks; the block now adjacent to each belongs wholly to the other function. Confirmed from content: the block above `assign_schema` explains `fixture`/`route` options that exist only in `synthesize_schema`'s body, and the `# Errors` paragraph matches `addressable_scope`'s fallible signature, not `repository_scope`'s infallible one.
+- Restoring the pre-merge state leaves `assign_schema` undocumented; authoring new documentation for it is explicitly out of scope.
+- Function order within each file is preserved; only documentation lines move, so no code line shifts position relative to any other code line.
+- The merge introduced exactly these two displacements in exactly these two files; no other site in the repository is in scope.
+- **Governance substitution**: no project constitution file exists in this checkout; the repository-root `AGENTS.md` was used in place of `/memory/constitution.md`. Its relevant constraints were honored: documentation is written in English, and the proof for this change is the smallest adequate check (per-site adjacency inspection plus a scoped formatting check), not a workspace-wide build.
